--- body ---

## Context

DECISIONS.md 2026-09-02, the standing correction entry, demotes two things from
ruling to probe, "both before 0566 commits the boundary". This is the second:
"the service's ceiling must be sized for two resident generations, since the
dual-embed window is days long; a ceiling pinned at one model rules D3 out by
arithmetic."

The gap is stated in SPEC.md §5.2.9 in the same words: of the three ceilings
the re-cut topology leaves needing a re-pin, "the embedding service has no
ceiling at all, being a class C3 did not have". The section also names the one
term nobody has measured on any machine — "the residency of a live batch —
every sweep on disk priced batch size in latency, not RSS" — which is exactly
the term a service ceiling is made of.

Two generations are not a corner case. SPEC.md §5.2.7 admits two coexisting
embedder generations across a model switch, and the service holds both under
one eviction rule; the ruling's own argument for the service is that this makes
dual-embed CHEAPER than the in-process arrangement, where every P0 would pay
for both. That argument is arithmetic, and the arithmetic has no measured
input.

## What it measures

Peak RSS of ONE service process, in four cells:

1. One generation resident, at rest.
2. One generation resident, with a live batch in flight at the ratified
   quantum's batch size.
3. Two generations resident, at rest — the incumbent MiniLM entry plus one
   multilingual candidate at its 8-bit rung.
4. Two generations resident, with a live batch in flight.

Cell 4 is the number the ceiling has to carry, and cells 1 to 3 are what make
it readable: the delta from 3 to 4 is the live-batch term §5.2.9 says nobody
has measured, and the delta from 2 to 4 is the price of the dual-embed window.
Record RSS with a real batch in flight rather than at rest, which is the
methodological point the section makes and the reason an existing latency sweep
cannot be re-read for this.

## On which machine

- **Binding arm**: the reference machine — laptop-class x86-64, four cores, no
  GPU, in the runtime the implementation ships (SPEC.md §5.2.8). C3's ceilings
  are asserted there, so a service ceiling measured anywhere else is not the
  ceiling.
- **Second configuration**: the disclosed GPU host, reported beside it and
  never as a substitute, per the same section's standing rule.
- The candidate models are already on disk from the registry sweeps; this probe
  adds no new download and no new candidate.

## Decision rule

- **Cell 4 fits under what C3 can carry on the reference machine** — the
  measured peak, with its headroom, is proposed to DECISIONS.md as the service
  ceiling, and the two other ceilings §5.2.9 lists as needing a re-pin gain a
  measured basis instead of a derivation.
- **Cell 4 does not fit** — then a ceiling sized for one model is what the
  machine actually affords, and that rules out SPEC.md §5.2.7's dual-residency
  by arithmetic exactly as the ruling warns. The choice among the remedies —
  shorten the dual-embed window, evict the old generation eagerly, forbid dual
  residency, or move the rung — is the author's, and goes to DECISIONS.md as a
  question with the four cells attached, not to code as a workaround.
- Either way, finding F1 gets its re-check: SPEC.md §5.2.9 says F1's collision
  between the pipeline ceiling and the multilingual candidates "may dissolve
  rather than be ruled" now that the worker holds no model. This probe's cell 2
  is what tells the difference, and "may dissolve" stays a re-check until it
  does.

## Measurement, 2026-09-02, on the reference machine

Ran by `bench/service_ceiling_rss.mjs`; artifact
`bench/results/0577-service-ceiling/service-ceiling-rss.json`. Host doudou,
Intel i5-8250U at 1,6 GHz — the binding arm, since C3's ceilings are asserted
there. Peak from `/proc/self/status` `VmHWM`, the kernel high-water mark, not a
sampler. **One fresh process per cell, five of them**, and the median reported
with its spread: within a single process `VmHWM` is monotone, so cells measured
in sequence would each inherit the previous one's peak and the 3→4 delta — the
live-batch term §5.2.9 says nobody has measured — would be unreadable.

Generation A is the incumbent, `Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2`, loaded exactly as the
shipped provider loads it (`pipeline('feature-extraction', model)` with no dtype
key, which resolves to the fp32 ONNX file). Generation B is
`Xenova/multilingual-e5-base` at its 8-bit rung, `q8`, the candidate the C3
ruling of 2026-08-30 recommends. Weights were pre-downloaded by a separate warm
pass, so no cell window holds a download.

**Batch size, derived rather than assumed.** DECISIONS.md 2026-09-01 ratifies a
time quantum of about 1 s with the size derived from it per device, so a fixed
size taken from anywhere else would measure a constant this project deliberately
did not ratify. Derived here, in a process of its own so the sweep's own peak
never lands in a cell: **16 passages** for the incumbent (1 088,5 ms) and
**4 passages** for the candidate at q8 (896,6 ms).

| cell | resident | in flight | peak RSS | spread |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | incumbent | — | **301,1 MB** | 4,7 |
| 2 | incumbent | batch of 16 | **464,7 MB** | 0,7 |
| 3 | incumbent + candidate q8 | — | **958,4 MB** | 10,9 |
| 4 | incumbent + candidate q8 | batch of 4 on the candidate | **1 002,5 MB** | 68,8 |
| 4b | incumbent + candidate q8 | a batch on each at once | **1 158,6 MB** | 1,5 |
| 1b | candidate q8 alone | — | **861,8 MB** | 4,0 |
| 2b | candidate q8 alone | batch of 4 | **910,1 MB** | 3,8 |

The deltas the cells were built to expose:

- **the live-batch term**, one generation: **+163,6 MB** (incumbent, batch of 16).
  This is the term §5.2.9 says every sweep on disk priced in latency and never in
  RSS. It is now priced.
- **the second generation at rest**: **+657,3 MB**.
- **the live-batch term with two resident**: **+44,1 MB** — smaller than the
  first because the candidate's derived batch is 4 where the incumbent's is 16,
  which is the quantum doing its job, not the model being cheaper.
- **the dual-embed window's price** (cell 4 against cell 2): **+537,8 MB**.
- **both batches in flight** (4b against 3): **+200,2 MB**.

**A methodological finding, which the first run got wrong.** The cells were at
first settled only where they were "at rest", and those read about 160 MB ABOVE
the batch cells that were not settled — cell 4 came out *below* cell 3 and the
live-batch delta was negative. The settle is now uniform, two seconds in every
cell, and a control arm at zero settle is reported beside the cells: 250,5 /
393,6 / 710,9 / 767,6 MB for cells 1 to 4. So a process that has just loaded a
model keeps growing its resident set for a second or two afterwards, by roughly
200 MB with both generations loaded. **The cause is not established** — arena
warm-up in the ONNX runtime, GC timing and lazy backend initialisation are all
consistent with it and none is confirmed. The consequence is firm and is what
the numbers rest on: a residency read taken immediately after a load understates
the settled figure, and it is the settled figure a ceiling has to carry.

That is also the most likely reconciliation with `bench/results/0263-cpu-arm`,
which puts multilingual-e5-base at q8 at 572,6 MB median delta. Cell 1b reads
861,8 MB total here; the no-settle control regime is where the two are
comparable. Stated as a reconciliation to check, not as a correction to 0263.

**Provenance of the committed artifact.** After the run the driver was changed
to name both models by registry id and to take pooling and normalize from
`bench/models.json` rather than as literals, which is what `bench/check_models.py`
asks for. The change is value-neutral here and deliberately so: both models
declare `pooling: mean` and `normalize: true`, and the resolved repository
strings are the ones the artifact already records, so the committed cells are
exactly what the committed code produces. Said rather than assumed, because a
driver edited after its artifact is the ordinary way provenance rots.

## The GPU host, 2026-09-03 — a second configuration, never a substitute

SPEC.md §5.2.8 admits a disclosed GPU host only as a second configuration
"reported beside it and never as a substitute", and C3's ceilings stay asserted
on the reference machine. Nothing below displaces the cells above: the binding
arm is the doudou table, and this is the comparison that lets the question
"would a GPU host rescue the ceiling?" have an answer instead of a guess. It
does not.

The author stopped padme's resident `llama-server`, which is what had left the
earlier arm with no device. Both cards were free; the run was pinned to device 0,
an NVIDIA RTX A4000 with 16 376 MiB, driver 580.159.04, under onnxruntime-node
1.24.3 and `@huggingface/transformers` 4.2.0.

### First, the question that had to be settled before any cell was worth running

**The shipped runtime does not use a GPU.** Measured, not read off
`embeddings.ts`. In the same run whose cells hold device memory, two control
cells differing from cell 1 only in the device argument report **301,0 MB** of
host RSS with no device memory at all — the process never appears in
`nvidia-smi`'s compute-app list — for the shipped call (`pipeline()` with no
device key, which is how `fork/src/features/search/embeddings.ts` loads a model),
and **300,9 MB**, again with none, for an explicit `device: 'cpu'`. The cell
beside them, differing only by `device: 'cuda'`, holds 547,7 MB of host RSS and
318 MB of VRAM. Both arms of the detector fired in one session on one host, so
the null is a measurement rather than a probe that could not look.

That control was itself wrong once, and the last section of this ticket records
how it was caught, because the way it failed is the reason to distrust the
version of it you did not check.

**Reaching the GPU takes more than an option.** `device: 'cuda'` alone fails
here: onnxruntime-node ships `libonnxruntime_providers_cuda.so` but not the cuDNN
it links against, and the load aborts with `Failed to load library …
libcudnn.so.9: cannot open shared object file`. cuDNN 9 is not installed
system-wide on this host; the arm ran with `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` pointing at the
author's own `~/opt/cudnn/lib`, which the artifact records beside every number.
The second configuration is therefore a host configuration and not a flag, and it
is disclosed as one.

### The cells

Same corpus as the reference-machine run (sha256 `e77b4c6f…`, 256 passages),
same code, same two generations, five fresh processes per cell, a uniform
two-second settle, and a zero-settle control arm beside it. Artifact
`bench/results/0577-service-ceiling/service-ceiling-rss-gpu-host-cuda.json`.

| cell | resident | in flight | peak host RSS | peak VRAM | spread |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| cuda-1 | incumbent | — | **547,7 MB** | **318 MB** | 1,1 |
| cuda-2 | incumbent | batch of 128 | **950,9 MB** | **2 758 MB** | 22,0 |
| cuda-3 | incumbent + candidate q8 | — | **1 031,4 MB** | **572 MB** | 10,1 |
| cuda-4 | incumbent + candidate q8 | batch of 8 on the candidate | **1 336,7 MB** | **842 MB** | 18,9 |
| cuda-4b | incumbent + candidate q8 | a batch on each at once | **1 472,2 MB** | **3 288 MB** | 3,6 |
| cuda-1b | candidate q8 alone | — | **1 025,7 MB** | **686 MB** | 17,1 |
| cuda-2b | candidate q8 alone | batch of 8 | **1 315,3 MB** | **696 MB** | 9,8 |

Host RSS and VRAM are different quantities, not two readings of one. Under a GPU
provider the weights and the activations sit in device memory, which the host
high-water mark no longer covers, so the two columns are never added and never
compared across devices. The VRAM column is the peak this process alone held,
read from `nvidia-smi`'s per-process accounting so that whatever else sits on the
card cannot enter it; the sampler polls at 0,2 s, and in the at-rest cells the
reps come back bimodal — cuda-3 reads 570 MB three times and 824 MB twice — so
that column carries less resolution than the RSS one and should not be read to
the megabyte.

The same machine's CPU, running the shipped call, is the host control: it is what
separates the machine from the device. Artifact `…-gpu-host-cpu.json`.

| cell | resident | in flight | peak host RSS | spread |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| cpu-1 | incumbent | — | **300,5 MB** | 1,3 |
| cpu-2 | incumbent | batch of 64 | **1 073,6 MB** | 3,7 |
| cpu-3 | incumbent + candidate q8 | — | **956,6 MB** | 16,8 |
| cpu-4 | incumbent + candidate q8 | batch of 8 on the candidate | **1 112,2 MB** | 13,9 |
| cpu-4b | incumbent + candidate q8 | a batch on each at once | **1 860,1 MB** | 3,6 |
| cpu-1b | candidate q8 alone | — | **864,4 MB** | 3,9 |
| cpu-2b | candidate q8 alone | batch of 8 | **1 003,4 MB** | 4,7 |

The batch sizes are derived per device from the ratified ~1 s quantum, as on the
reference machine and for the same reason: **128** and **8** passages under CUDA
(594,7 ms and 880,5 ms), **64** and **8** on this machine's CPU (1 178,1 ms and
824,8 ms), against 16 and 4 on the reference machine.

### What the second configuration says

**A GPU host raises the host ceiling; it does not relieve it.** Cell 4 is
1 336,7 MB of host RSS here against 1 002,5 MB on the reference machine, and it
holds 842 MB of VRAM the reference machine does not need. The CUDA context alone
costs about 247 MB of host memory before any batch — 547,7 MB at rest against
300,5 MB for the same model on the same machine's CPU. Against C3's ~750 MB the
reference machine's cell 4 is 34 % over and this one is 78 % over. The decision
rule's second branch fires harder on a GPU host, not softer, so "put the service
on a GPU host" is not among the remedies the author is choosing between.

**The rung C3 recommends gains nothing from the device.**
`multilingual-e5-base` at q8 derives the same batch of 8 under CUDA as on this
machine's CPU, and a batch takes 880,5 ms there against 824,8 ms on the CPU —
slower, not faster. The incumbent at fp32 does accelerate: a batch of 64 takes
1 178,1 ms on that CPU and 247,1 ms under CUDA, near five times faster. The
candidate is on the device — cuda-1b holds 686 MB of VRAM — and simply gains
nothing there. Per-node fallback on the quantized graph fits the shape of that;
nothing here establishes it, and it is left as a question rather than a cause.

**A ceiling in bytes is not portable while the batch size is derived from a time
quantum.** The at-rest cells agree across the two machines to within 1 % —
300,5 MB against the reference machine's 301,1, 956,6 against 958,4, 864,4
against 861,8 — so residency at rest is a property of the models and not of the
machine. What moves is the derived batch: 16 passages on the reference machine,
64 on this machine's CPU, 128 under CUDA, and the live-batch cell moves with it,
464,7 then 1 073,6 then 950,9 MB. A faster device earns a larger batch out of the
ratified ~1 s quantum and holds more memory for it, so **the reference machine's
figure is the fleet's floor rather than its bound**: the worst case measured
anywhere is cpu-4b at 1 860,1 MB, two and a half times C3. That is a consequence
of the 2026-09-01 quantum ruling meeting a ceiling expressed in bytes, and it is
put here as an observation, not a proposal.

**The post-load growth is reproduced and still unexplained.** Every cell above is
read after a uniform two-second settle, with the zero-settle control arm beside
it. Under CUDA the controls read: cuda-1 **538,5 MB**, cuda-2 **904,1 MB**,
cuda-3 **917,5 MB**, cuda-4 **1 134,9 MB**. On the same machine's CPU: cpu-1
**247,9 MB**, cpu-2 **1 027,8 MB**, cpu-3 **720,9 MB**, cpu-4 **874,2 MB**. So
the two-generation cells sit 236 and 238 MB above their controls on this
machine's CPU, against 248 and 235 MB on the reference machine — the same effect,
on a different machine and a different CPU vendor, at the same magnitude. It is
not an artifact of the reference machine. Under CUDA it nearly vanishes at rest
with one generation, 547,7 against 538,5, while the two-generation cells keep
most of it; and those are the cells holding the candidate, whose graph the same
run shows gaining nothing from the device. That narrows where to look. **It names
no cause, and none is proposed here.**

### A control that could not come out the other way, and how it was caught

The first version of the device-control arm reported 300 and 332 MB of VRAM for
cells it labelled "the shipped call" and "explicit cpu". Both had in fact run on
CUDA: `runCell` appended the run's own `--device cuda` after the control's
override, and `parseArgs` lets the last occurrence win, so the override was
itself silently overridden. Nothing failed. The arm measured precisely the thing
it existed to rule out and filed it under the other name — ticket 0481's defect,
moved from the cell into the control. It was caught only because a hand-run pair
on the same host had already established what the shipped call reports, so the
disagreement had something to disagree with. `runCell` now pushes caller
overrides last, and the committed run's two controls report no device memory
while every cell beside them holds VRAM.

## Verdict against the decision rule

**Cell 4 does not fit under what C3 carries.** C3 stands at ~750 MB p95
(DECISIONS.md 2026-08-30). Cell 4 is 1 002,5 MB, 34 % above it; the worst case
4b is 1 158,6 MB, 54 % above; and cell 3 — two generations merely resident, with
nothing in flight — is already 958,4 MB, 28 % above. The ticket's second branch
is therefore the live one: a ceiling sized for one model is what this machine
affords, and SPEC.md §5.2.7's dual residency is ruled out by arithmetic unless
something moves.

The choice among the remedies is the author's, and goes to DECISIONS.md as a
question with the cells attached, not to code as a workaround.

**F1's re-check, which cells 1b and 2b are what tells the difference.** F1 was
ruled by pinning the pipeline ceiling at ≤ ~750 MB on the argument that "the
pipeline worker is the smallest process that can embed — the model plus one
batch". That process is exactly cell 2b, and it measures **910,1 MB**: the
collision was real, and it was 21 % wide, not marginal. What dissolves it is the
service topology itself — under it the worker holds no model at all, so the
750 MB pipeline ceiling no longer has to cover one. The collision does not
dissolve; it *moves*, from the pipeline worker to the service, where cells 3 and
4 now price it. §5.2.9's "may dissolve rather than be ruled" is answered: it
dissolves for the worker and lands on the service.

## What was not run

**The GPU second configuration was not run on 2026-09-02** — padme's two GPUs
were fully held by a resident `llama-server` that could not be stopped, so that
arm had no device. It ran on 2026-09-03 once the author stopped it, and the
section above reports it. Nothing in the 2026-09-02 table was changed by it: the
binding arm is the reference machine, and the GPU host is reported beside it.

The registry was read for the candidate's identity only, and `bench/models.json`
was pinned by content hash
`e93913581ac0da70c765941453051225d3a133b1560c26444329fe36ddf04112` at read time,
since ticket 0490's registry work was in flight in the same window.

## Exit criteria
- [x] Four cells measured on the reference machine, RSS peak per cell, with the
      batch size and the rung recorded beside each number
- [x] The GPU host reported as a second configuration — measured 2026-09-03 on
      padme (RTX A4000, driver 580.159.04) under an explicit CUDA execution
      provider, with the same machine's CPU run beside it as the host control;
      artifacts `bench/results/0577-service-ceiling/service-ceiling-rss-gpu-host-cuda.json`
      and `…-gpu-host-cpu.json`. Reported beside the reference machine, never as
      a substitute for it
- [x] A committed summary under `bench/results/`, declared in the figure guard
      like every other measurement quoted in prose
- [x] A proposed service ceiling, or the arithmetic showing there is none, sent
      to DECISIONS.md as the author's ruling to make
- [x] Ticket 0026's RSS gate has a service arm it can assert against, and
      ticket 0575's boundary has a bound
